    “What were they looking for?”
    “Where the ground note is loudest. So they’d know where theirs
would hold.” A spoon against a cup. “The scientists have a number for
it, seven point something. Lightning ringing round the world. My
grandmother called it the ground note. Nobody owns it, and it doesn’t
go in a ledger. They only wanted to know where it was strong, so they
could build where it wasn’t.”
    “Would you come?” I said. I hadn’t known I was going to ask.
    “No.” No apology in it. “I don’t leave this ground, and I don’t stand
on anybody else’s without leave. That’s the whole of the rule, and it’s
why it’s held.” The kettle began to whistle. “When your night comes,
ring me. I’ll be where I can hear.”
    She hung up before I could thank her, which I would learn was how
she ended every call.



I don’t know who called her. I think it was Manny, who’s constitution‐
ally incapable of keeping a secret from a woman who cooks.
   Mamá arrived at seven in her church coat, holding a manila folder
against her chest with both hands, the way she holds a pot she doesn’t
trust the handle of. She didn’t say hello. She came to the wall, and
looked at the six names, and the faces, and went very white at the gray
man in the good coat.
   “Ese,” she said.
   “Má. You know him?”
   She didn’t answer. She opened the folder and drew out a single
sheet, soft with age, folded in thirds. She’d kept it in the top drawer of
the dresser for nineteen years. I’d seen the corner of it. I’d never seen it
open.
   She handed it to me, and I took one corner, and the room went.
   Our kitchen, 2007.




